# A `match` arm whose pattern opens with a `-` set apart from its literal
# (design.md §4.7, §4.15, §4.17; the spec's opening paragraph; defect 123).
#
# `k = match n` over `- 1 => 10` compiled and matched -1. A NEWLINE separates
# a `match` from its arms and each arm from the next, and the spec's opening
# paragraph says that where a NEWLINE separates without a `,`, the next line
# may not begin with a `-` that does not touch its operand. Panel 180 wrote
# that sentence with no bracket limit and refused the shape between a
# literal's elements (`parse/list_line`); panel 181 refused it leading a
# statement's line (`open_line`), and let a `-` that opens an arm through to
# the parser, which read it as the pattern's sign. The arm was the context
# left.
#
# **One sentence, one code**: `spaced_minus_element`, the list's, and on the
# thesis list with it (`diag.is_thesis_rule`), since without the rule the arm
# still has a meaning, the negative pattern.
#
# **The fixes follow the literal, and only a string's is `certain`** (panel
# 185 R6; design.md §4.15, and §4.8's *two readings make two guesses*).
# Before a string the `-` signs nothing, since `-"a"` is refused by its type,
# so the arm has one reading and the fix deletes the `-`. Before an integer or
# a character both readings are patterns: the negative literal, `-1`, the way
# `fmt` prints it, and a bullet, `1`, the habit of a list written as markdown.
# So both are offered and both are guesses. Until that sitting the sign was
# `certain` everywhere, on the premise that in a pattern a `-` can only be its
# literal's sign, and one bulleted arm beside `_` falsified it: `- 1 => "one"`
# over `_ => "many"` was applied into `-1` and printed `many` (its t7). Both
# fixes rewrite the `-` and whatever sets it apart, a space or a tab, so a
# tab there does not go into the margin.
#
# **Only the space is this module's.** A comment or a line end after the `-`
# ends the line with it, which outside brackets the lexer has refused
# already (`continuation_outside_brackets`), and a second diagnostic here
# would count one break twice. Out of the expression knot for
# `parse/list_line`'s reason: a question with a name and no cycle.

use token
use diag
use cursor
use parse/line_end

# At an arm's first pattern: refuse it where it opens with a `-` that a space
# sets apart from its operand, on the line the `-` begins. Nothing is
# consumed, so the pattern is read as it always was, the negative literal,
# and the parse goes on.
function spaced_sign(@c: cursor.Cursor, text: str)
    if !cursor.at(c, .minus)
        return
    sign = cursor.current_span(c)
    operand = c.tokens[c.pos + 1]

    if operand.span.start == sign.end || !opens_its_line(text, at: sign.start) || !signs(operand.kind)
        return

    if line_end.has_a_line_end(text.slice(from: sign.end, to: operand.span.start))
        return
    apart = token.Span(start: sign.start, end: operand.span.start)
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "spaced_minus_element",
        message: "this arm begins with `- `, a minus set apart from its value by a space, and where a NEWLINE separates, as it does every arm from the line above, the next line may not begin with a `-` that does not touch its operand: " + reading(operand.kind),
        span: sign
    )

    if operand.kind == .str_lit
        d.fixes @ d.fixes.push(
            diag.Fix(title: "delete the `-`, which signs no string", replacement: "", span: apart, certainty: .certain)
        )
    else
        d.fixes @ d.fixes.push(
            diag.Fix(title: "a negative literal: write the `-` against its value", replacement: "-", span: apart, certainty: .guess)
        )
        d.fixes @ d.fixes.push(
            diag.Fix(title: "a bullet: delete the `-`", replacement: "", span: apart, certainty: .guess)
        )
    cursor.say(@c, d)

# What the `-` can be before this literal, the tail of the message.
function reading(kind: token.TokenKind) -> str
    if kind == .str_lit
        return "before a string the `-` signs nothing, so delete it"
    return "the `-` is the literal's sign or a bullet, and nothing in the arm says which, so write it against its value, or delete it"

# A literal spec § 8's `Pattern` writes after a `-`: an integer, a string, a
# character, the string's sign refused by its type, which is why its fix is
# the deletion. After anything else the `-` begins no pattern, which
# `parse/arm_pattern` refuses (defect 124), so a second diagnostic here
# would count one mistake twice; and a comment after the `-` ends its
# line, which is the lexer's.
function signs(kind: token.TokenKind) -> bool
    return kind == .int_lit || kind == .str_lit || kind == .char_lit

# Whether only the margin's spaces stand between the line's start and `at`.
function opens_its_line(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    i: i64 @ at

    while i > 0 && text[i - 1] == ' '
        i @ i - 1

    return i == 0 || text[i - 1] == '\n'
